  "worked_solution": "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\u27f6[\\begin{aligned}x-y \\\\ x+y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $x+y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2}+x+y=0\\,\u27f9\\,(x-y)^{2}+(x+y)=0\\,\u27f9\\,v=-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x-y=u \\\\ x+y=v\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,y=\\frac{v-u}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the equation of the given line yields:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\, & \u27f9\\,\\frac{v-u}{2}=0 & \\,\u27f9\\,v=u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, to find the intersections of the resulting lines in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v=-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=u\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}u(u+1)=0 \\\\ v=u.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(-1,-1).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073263/assets/05d7e65dca3c6d116d01be9e57fc83f3e3edcda4135a3f96fea82af8db4f2d1e.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 0,\\,u\\le v\\le -{u}^{2}}.\n$$",
